  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Sagaing: Humanitarian support for children and their families through WASH, Shelter/NFIs and Child Protection interventions</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="2">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">ltpgtltspan style=&quot;font-size: 14px&quot;gtSave the Children International (SCI), in partnership with
Ar Yone Oo Social Development Association (AYO-SDA), will deliver a 12-month
multi-sectoral project to meaningfully contribute to the well-being of affected
children, their families, and communities from 17 villages in Sagaing. This
will be achieved through child protection, shelter/NFI, and WASH interventions.
SCI and its partner will provide child protection case management services,
including identification and referral services, capacity strengthening of the
case management workforce, alternative care support for unaccompanied and
separated children, provision of referrals to legal aid support services, and
family tracing and reunification for unaccompanied or separated children.
Additionally, SCI will provide MHPSS services for girls and boys affected by
the ongoing political crisis and conflict and their families, along with
outreach and prevention activities. SCI and its partner will prioritize the
provision of safe drinking water through water point renovation and water
quality monitoring. SCI will also install gender-segregated and
disability-inclusive latrines and bathing facilities, provide solid waste
management services, and distribute hygiene items. Hygiene promotion sessions
will be conducted at the community level through SCI&#x2019;s partner. SCI will supply
NFI kits, including clothing, bedding, and cooking items, as well as materials
for shelter repairs, such as tarpaulins and rope. SCI will provide necessary
technical support to the partner, including capacity building. SCI will also
regularly monitor the partner&apos;s implementation and work jointly to ensure that
planned interventions are implemented in a timely and quality manner. Inclusive
aspects of programming, such as gender-responsive, disability-inclusive, child-sensitive,
and the inclusion of PSS, will be mainstreamed in all proposed activities.
Community and child-friendly (IEC) tools on explosive ordnance risks will also
be rolled out, where appropriate, to promote awareness.lt/spangtltbrgtlt/pgtltpgt





ltbrgtlt/pgt</narrative>
